    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `compress_for_rename`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `compress_for_rename` function is designed to determine which paths need to be renamed during the uninstallation process of packages. It takes an iterable of file paths as input and returns a set of paths that should be renamed. The function creates a case-insensitive mapping of paths and iterates over the directories to check if all files within a directory are part of the paths to be removed. If so, it adds a wildcard for the directory to the set of paths to be renamed and removes the individual files from consideration. The function relies on the `os.walk` method to traverse directories and the `os.path` module for path manipulations. It handles case insensitivity by normalizing paths with `os.path.normcase`. The function does not raise exceptions or handle specific edge cases; it assumes the input paths are valid and exist on disk. It has no side effects, as it only processes and returns data without modifying the filesystem.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12